{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head b4d060ba2ab7a26cfc78c28bc7a064585e786067 on planner/state-seed-note-20261113, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-13-state-seed.md`, for request 4893fbfa under promise dfff3158 and review request c6eafabb. The rule of section 3 is right, and tying the seed to the floor is right. Four statements that the design rests on are wrong at main 57e893c0. Correct them before the note lands, because section 9 binds build step C2 to them now.\n\nRequired change 1: the readers of `Own` (section 3).\n\n- The note says the fold and the judges read earlier entries in four places, and that each reader takes a missing entry as absent with no fault. About twenty readers exist in `packages/derive/src`, and they do four different things with a missing entry:\n  - Absent, with no fault: `equal` (`operand.ts:665-666`), `program/guard-value.ts:43-44`, `entryOf` (`operand.ts:466-476`) and the opener in `outcomes.ts:260`.\n  - Refuse or answer `unavailable`: the local fact field (`operand.ts:371-376`, `fact-mismatch`), the `fact` guard (`program/guard-value.ts:124-128`), `send-value.ts:19-20` with `sends.ts:293` (`send-unresolved`), `prepare.ts:231`, `capability/hold.ts:817`, `capability/device-command.ts:71,83` and `judgeFloor` (`floor.ts:169-175`).\n  - Throw: `settle.ts:235-237` (`RuleFault`), `nextFloorState` through `stateKept` (`floor.ts:137`), and `fold.ts:305` at a floor entry with no `Own`.\n  - Skip, with no trace: `priorDetachedBounds` (`handlers.ts:134-141`) loses a text size bound, `capability/gitread.ts:72` drops its check, and the size-proof pass of `nextFloorState` skips a missing entry (`floor.ts:142-143`).\n- `fixedRowsOf` reads `fixedHash` from the state, not `Own` (`fold.ts:301-314`, `state.ts:408-419`). `Own` also carries `sequence()`, `retained`, `positions`, `sizeProofs` and `archived` (`fields.ts:28-36`), and `Own.retained` and `context.retained` read foreign copies (`floor.ts:125`, `settle.ts:239`). `marks.ts:502` hands `Own` to platform rules, so the set of readers is open. `operand.ts:157,169,179` compare `Own` by identity, so a replica must keep one `Own` object across phases.\n- The conclusion stands and grows stronger: a replica that lacks an entry can refuse where the scope admits, admit where the scope refuses (the lost text bound), or throw. State the rule without the claim that every reader treats absence alike, replace the table or mark it as a partial list, and keep section 9 item 3, which makes the builder's list authoritative.\n\nRequired change 2: the loader (section 4.1).\n\n- `fixedRowsOf` alone does not rebuild the fixed index. The loader must pass each result to `putFixed`, as `sqlite.ts:229` does. The fixed hashes, rows, counts and dependencies live outside `StateSnapshot`.\n- `SqliteStore.all` (`sqlite.ts:540-573`) builds a `MemoryState` only to produce a snapshot. It calls neither `putFixedHash` nor `indexItems`, and nothing folds over its result. \"SqliteStore loads a MemoryState this way from its tables today\" overstates the precedent.\n- Foreign retained copies and detached texts that the judges read are outside the snapshot too. Say how a loaded replica gets the copies that `Own.retained` serves, or state that a judge that needs one answers `unavailable` in the replica.\n\nRequired change 3: what the seed shows a member (section 6).\n\n- The note says the seed \"shows a member nothing that `log`, `summary` and `items` do not show\". That holds for held entries: a member session has no limit (`reads.ts:144`), and `log` returns each held entry whole (`reads.ts:313`). It fails below the floor. `log` answers `archived` there (`reads.ts:311`), and `summary` and `items` show only items and counts (`reads.ts:228-270`). The seed carries what archived entries left in the state: `accepted` (actor, key and intent digest of each act), `records`, `requests`, `decided`, `creations`, `relations`, `observed`, `holders`, `accounts`, `prepared` and `sourceOrder`.\n- State the real rule: a member's session reads the state that archived entries left, including the accepted keys of other members. Say whether that matches who may read the archive. If it does, the read adds nothing. If it does not, the seed widens a member's read, and that is the planner's decision to state.\n\nRequired change 4: the genesis entry (section 8).\n\n- Section 8 lists \"the genesis entry, when position 0 is archived\" as a thing the client cannot check. `stateKept` always keeps position 0 (`floor.ts:86`, `add(0)`), and `entry` serves a kept position below the floor (`floor.ts:26-30`, `reads.ts:341-347`). A client can always read and check the genesis entry. Remove the limit, and add the genesis check to section 7.\n\nThe kernel question. I confirm the reading of section 10. `loadState` uses `MemoryState` and its `put` methods, `indexItems`, `fixedRowsOf`, `putFixed` and `StateSnapshot`, which `packages/derive` exports today. The seed is runtime storage that replay does not read. The read sits outside `ScopeApi`. The note names no K item. Section 16 point O3, a floor entry that names a seed digest, would need section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`, and the note leaves it there.\n\nWhat I read: the whole note, and the source behind sections 3, 4, 6, 7 and 8 at 57e893c0. Section 7 step 5 holds: `log` answers `archived` with the floor and `next: F` (`reads.ts:311`), and `entry` serves kept positions below it. Section 8 holds on the checkpoint: `checkpoint` is not in `ROOM_RPC_METHODS` (`scope/src/worker.ts:256-260`), and replay never takes one as proof (`replay/src/verify.ts:2560-2562`).\n\nWhat I checked: the head stands on f0854d8f, the client note's head, and adds one path. The artifact covers it at the head and is neither stale nor retired. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, the commit-message check passes (1 checked, 0 flagged), and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests, since the head changes no code.\n\nArchitecture: sound apart from the four corrections. The seed belongs to the floor because only a floor makes the replica unable to start. One seed for each floor bounds the storage to one copy of the state. A replica that runs across a floor entry drops what the scope drops, so a floor never stops a running replica.\n\nSecurity: required change 3 is the one finding. The refusal of a signed read is right: a signed reader's `log` holds only its own entries and their causes, and the state holds every entry's effect. Two notes for SS2:\n1. Section 5.1 makes the seed after the turn that seals the floor entry. A read can arrive between that turn and the copy pass and get the old seed, whose tail the floor has dropped. The client restarts and then succeeds, so nothing breaks, but making the seed in the floor's own turn closes the window.\n2. The new member read name `seed` changes the entry-point inventory (`scripts/security-entry-points.mjs`) and its seal.\n\nSimplification: no required change. `ScopeObject.checkpoint()` stays a public Durable Object method (`object.ts:654`) that no route calls. Section 15 decision 8 keeps the checkpoint unused, and its removal would make that decision visible in code.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cf75177ab3fb3f4a2a67dd96f26b44d630d6bffe","binding":"assigned","head":"b4d060ba2ab7a26cfc78c28bc7a064585e786067","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:da27a2f886472ae0913d6dd3d686fdcf31c80c33\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dfff31586bb07ff7e0c0be591c9674a11f5af190\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:98a502c5dc5613e9ba2dc4d8deb557e7b405bf9e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}